"Organizes external macro-environmental forces into six domains to examine their strategic implications over a stated horizon.\n\nUse when: Explore external drivers before entering a market. Review how contextual change could affect an existing strategy.\n\nAvoid when: Internal operational strengths or direct competitor rivalry are the main question.\n\nVisual grammar: Six labeled regions separate political, economic, social, technological, legal and environmental factors. Notes identify specific changes and their implications. Time and evidence tags distinguish present conditions from speculative developments.\n\nBuild: Define the organization, geography and planning horizon. Gather credible signals within each external domain. Translate significant signals into implications, uncertainties and monitoring questions.\n\nCheck: Factors are external to the organization. Legal statements retain the relevant jurisdiction and date. Repeated cross-domain drivers are linked or referenced consistently.\n\nPitfalls: Listing broad topic words without explaining their consequences. Treating an uncertain forecast as an established external fact.\n\nExample: A bicycle retailer scans transport policy, household spending, commuting habits, battery advances, sales rules and extreme-weather exposure.\n\nAlternatives: SWOT analysis, Porter Five Forces."
